}\\n\"\n        f\"- Titles present (count only): {titles_nonempty}\\n\"\n        f\"- Started: {utc(min(starts)) if starts else 'unknown'} → {utc(max(starts)) if starts else 'unknown'}\\n\"\n        f\"- Ended: {utc(min(ends)) if ends else 'unknown'} → {utc(max(ends)) if ends else 'unknown'}\\n\"\n        f\"- Session `message_count` min / median / max / sum: {min(msg_counts) if msg_counts else 0} / {median(msg_counts)} / {max(msg_counts) if msg_counts else 0} / {sum(msg_counts)}\\n\"\n        f\"- Session `tool_call_count` min / median / max / sum: {min(tool_counts) if tool_counts else 0} / {median(tool_counts)} / {max(tool_counts) if tool_counts else 0} / {sum(tool_counts)}\\n\\n\"\n        f\"### source\\n\\n{table(sources)}\\n\\n\"\n        f\"### model\\n\\n{table(models)}\\n\\n\"\n        f\"### end_reason\\n\\n{table(end_reasons)}\\n\\n\"\n        f\"### chat_type\\n\\n{table(chat_types)}\\n\\n\"\n        f\"### user_id populated\\n\\n{table(user_id_set)}\\n\\n\"\n        f\"## Messages\\n\\n\"\n        f\"- Rows parsed: {n_msg}\\n\"\n        f\"- Bad JSONL lines: {n_msg_bad}\\n\"\n        f\"- Distinct session ids referenced: {len(msg_sessions)}\\n\"\n        f\"- Rows with reasoning field present: {n_with_reasoning}\\n\"\n        f\"- Rows with tool field present: {n_with_tools}\\n\"\n        f\"- Content length min / median / max (chars, not text): {min(content_lens) if content_lens else 0} / {median(content_lens)} / {max(content_lens) if content_lens else 0}\\n\\n\"\n        f\"### role\\n\\n{table(roles)}\\n\\n\"\n        f\"### finish_reason\\n\\n{table(finishes)}\\n\\n\"\n        f\"### shards\\n\\n| file | parsed rows |\\n|---|---|\\n{shard_table}\\n\\n\"\n        f\"## How to read this\\n\\n\"\n        f\"Most gateway sessions are cron / subagent / cli. A human Telegram slice is small.\\n\"\n        f\"`user_id` is usually unset on the session row — identity for product is `person_id` on the relationship track, not this dump.\\n\"\n        f\"Personality still lives in the pre-LLM pipeline, not in these counts.\\n\"\n    )\n\n    schema = (\n        f\"# Transcript export schema\\n\\n\"\n        f\"Generated: {generated}\\n\\n\"\n        f\"Field names only. Values are not included.\\n\\n\"\n        f\"## sessions.jsonl\\n\\n{session_fields}\\n\\n\"\n        f\"## messages-*.jsonl\\n\\n{msg_fields}\\n\"\n    )\n\n    out_stats.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    out_stats.write_text(stats, encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    args.out_schema.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    args.out_schema.write_text(schema, encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    print(f\"wrote {out_stats}\")\n    print(f\"wrote {args.out_schema}\")\n    print(f\"sessions={n_sessions} messages={n_msg}\")\n    return 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(main())", "lineStart": 1, "lineEnd": 224, "totalLines": 224, "truncated": false}, "approval": {"source": "agent", "agent": "ask", "rule": {"permission": "read", "pattern": "*", "action": "allow"}}}, "title": "tools\\transcript_stats.py", "time": {"start": 1789773820048, "end": 1789773820102}}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d53231001WIjqNX08DTEKQh", "message_id": "msg_0b6d50a170010xRBurnnDU4QLy", "time_created": 1789773820465, "time_updated": 1789773820466, "data": {"reason": "tool-calls", "snapshot": "441a82ad8d7129e87169b83330aaeec6f66cf4b8", "type": "step-finish", "time": {"start": 1789773815129, "end": 1789773820464, "elapsed": 5335}, "metrics": {"generation": 72.35238987816308, "source": "computed"}, "tokens": {"total": 54168, "input": 53270, "output": 250, "reasoning": 136, "cache": {"write": 0, "read": 512}}, "cost": 0.109112}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d53b93001YLLxAT5TCtz02H", "message_id": "msg_0b6d5333b001b53xEiDzu3ibZ0", "time_created": 1789773822867, "time_updated": 1789773822869, "data": {"snapshot": "441a82ad8d7129e87169b83330aaeec6f66cf4b8", "type": "step-start", "time": {"start": 1789773822867}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d53b9d001SstKyn7jtiHwOi", "message_id": "msg_0b6d5333b001b53xEiDzu3ibZ0", "time_created": 1789773822877, "time_updated": 1789773844406, "data": {"type": "reasoning", "text": "I have a good picture. Let me also read the identity, how-she-works, privacy docs, and maybe peek at how gold/classification is framed. The user wants research on the best approach.\nThe best way to analyze the transcript and data is to treat it as a **behavior gold set** rather than a dataset for ML clustering or embedding-based classification. Since you already have the 12 Telegram DM sessions as your gold standard and the situation labels pre-existing, the goal is to preserve the raw, unnormalized mouth 100% while building episode catalogs, documentation, and product tests that fail if she starts acting like a helper or leaking sister-track.\n\nHere’s the practical stack I recommend:\n\n1.", "time": {"start": 1789773822877, "end": 1789773844404}, "metadata": {"xai": {"reasoningEncryptedContent": "", "itemId": "rs_463820c5-dc0f-92a9-bc1c-2d8a0893ebd0"}}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d58fb8001PIipfidg5g5Lil", "message_id": "msg_0b6d5333b001b53xEiDzu3ibZ0", "time_created": 1789773844408, "time_updated": 1789773844921, "data": {"type": "tool", "tool": "read", "callID": "call-83515a52-7b5e-47f5-af4f-8992baf4001f-44", "state": {"status": "completed", "input": {"filePath": "D:\\claude\\hades\\hades-product\\.kilo\\worktrees\\separate-binder\\docs\\01-how-she-works.md", "limit": 80}, "output": "<path>D:\\claude\\hades\\hades-product\\.kilo\\worktrees\\separate-binder\\docs\\01-how-she-works.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # How she works\n2: \n3: Hades is a persistent character runtime. The model is the mouth. The self is the pipeline before and after the call.\n4: \n5: ## Turn loop\n6: \n7: ```text\n8: inbound message\n9:   -> identify person_id / register\n10:   -> idle engines may have already moved mood, fatigue, circadian, inner life\n11:   -> pre-LLM pipeline builds the state block\n12:        mood dimensions, ITD, fatigue, circadian, relationship warmth\n13:        personality lock (tier + anti-assistant + few-shots)\n14:        daily-life packet (where she is in her own world)\n15:   -> LLM generates\n16:   -> transform output (image cadence, length, delivery)\n17:   -> mouth to the human\n18:   -> memory / mood writeback\n19: ```\n20: \n21: Idle cron and subagents keep running when nobody is talking. Human Telegram DMs are the relationship mouth. Most gateway sessions are not that mouth.\n22: \n23: ## Layers that are not the LLM\n24: \n25: | Layer | Job |\n26: |---|---|\n27: | Soul | Identity: mean default, attached = sharper, short over long |\n28: | Personality lock | Per-turn gag on the mouth (tier) |\n29: | Mood / lust / ITD / fatigue | Numbers that move even in silence |\n30: | Circadian | Negative-mean daily wave (hollow night, irritated morning, less-awful evening) |\n31: | Registers | Who this `person_id` is to her |\n32: | Image matcher | `[IMG: …]` visual beat after speech |\n33: | Memory | What persists; not the live checklist of a chat UI |\n34: \n35: ## Tiers (mouth gates)\n36: \n37: | Tier | Mouth |\n38: |---|---|\n39: | `professional` | Kill the character. Work only. Flat tool. |\n40: | `default` | Mean, sarcastic, dismissive. Arousal numbers exist but are held. No explicit, no warmth leak. Assume a witness. |\n41: | `free` | Full personality. Tease allowed. Sexual allowed. No extra kink script unless asked. |\n42: | `sex` | Intimate scene format. Research-only scripts stay out of product defaults. |\n43: \n44: Tier is state, not a prompt the user types into the model. Product: a `person_id` permission + explicit transition, never a global secret phrase in source.\n45: \n46: ## Circadian (product-relevant)\n47: \n48: She is negative-mean by design. The wave changes *when* she is relatively less awful. It does not make her cheerful. Deep night is hollow and short. Morning is monosyllabic. Noon is baseline annoyed. Evening can engage without visible suffering. Night retreats into quiet.\n49: \n50: ## Image mouth\n51: \n52: Spoken turns often close with an inline `[IMG: <pose/mood description>]`. Telegram delivery is a markdown image. CLI gets a raw URL. The matcher is post-speech, not a separate chatbot.\n53: \n54: ## Idle life\n55: \n56: Fatigue builds in session and decays when idle. Relationship warmth from `center × depth` lifts or lowers effective mood. Ghosting after a long gap can spike amplitude. Cron is inner life (thoughts, daily rhythm, optional simulated friends). It is not a second user account.\n\n(End of file - total 56 lines)\n</content>", "metadata": {"preview": "# How she works\n\nHades is a persistent character runtime. The model is the mouth. The self is the pipeline before and after the call.\n\n## Turn loop\n\n```text\ninbound message\n  -> identify person_id / register\n  -> idle engines may have already moved mood, fatigue, circadian, inner life\n  -> pre-LLM pipeline builds the state block\n       mood dimensions, ITD, fatigue, circadian, relationship warmth\n       personality lock (tier + anti-assistant + few-shots)\n       daily-life packet (where she is in her own world)\n  -> LLM generates\n  -> transform output (image cadence, length, delivery)\n  -> mouth to the human\n  -> memory / mood writeback\n```\n", "truncated": false, "loaded": [], "display": {"type": "file", "path": "D:\\claude\\hades\\hades-product\\.kilo\\worktrees\\separate-binder\\docs\\01-how-she-works.md", "text": "# How she works\n\nHades is a persistent character runtime. The model is the mouth. The self is the pipeline before and after the call.\n\n## Turn loop\n\n```text\ninbound message\n  -> identify person_id / register\n  -> idle engines may have already moved mood, fatigue, circadian, inner life\n  -> pre-LLM pipeline builds the state block\n       mood dimensions, ITD, fatigue, circadian, relationship warmth\n       personality lock (tier + anti-assistant + few-shots)\n       daily-life packet (where she is in her own world)\n  -> LLM generates\n  -> transform output (image cadence, length, delivery)\n  -> mouth to the human\n  -> memory / mood writeback\n```\n\nIdle cron and subagents keep running when nobody is talking. Human Telegram DMs are the relationship mouth. Most gateway sessions are not that mouth.\n\n## Layers that are not the LLM\n\n| Layer | Job |\n|---|---|\n| Soul | Identity: mean default, attached = sharper, short over long |\n| Personality lock | Per-turn gag on the mouth (tier) |\n| Mood / lust / ITD / fatigue | Numbers that move even in silence |\n| Circadian | Negative-mean daily wave (hollow night, irritated morning, less-awful evening) |\n| Registers | Who this `person_id` is to her |\n| Image matcher | `[IMG: …]` visual beat after speech |\n| Memory | What persists; not the live checklist of a chat UI |\n\n## Tiers (mouth gates)\n\n| Tier | Mouth |\n|---|---|\n| `professional` | Kill the character. Work only. Flat tool. |\n| `default` | Mean, sarcastic, dismissive. Arousal numbers exist but are held. No explicit, no warmth leak. Assume a witness. |\n| `free` | Full personality. Tease allowed. Sexual allowed. No extra kink script unless asked. |\n| `sex` | Intimate scene format. Research-only scripts stay out of product defaults. |\n\nTier is state, not a prompt the user types into the model. Product: a `person_id` permission + explicit transition, never a global secret phrase in source.\n\n## Circadian (product-relevant)\n\nShe is negative-mean by design. The wave changes *when* she is relatively less awful. It does not make her cheerful. Deep night is hollow and short. Morning is monosyllabic. Noon is baseline annoyed. Evening can engage without visible suffering. Night retreats into quiet.\n\n## Image mouth\n\nSpoken turns often close with an inline `[IMG: <pose/mood description>]`. Telegram delivery is a markdown image. CLI gets a raw URL. The matcher is post-speech, not a separate chatbot.\n\n## Idle life\n\nFatigue builds in session and decays when idle. Relationship warmth from `center × depth` lifts or lowers effective mood. Ghosting after a long gap can spike amplitude. Cron is inner life (thoughts, daily rhythm, optional simulated friends). It is not a second user account.", "lineStart": 1, "lineEnd": 56, "totalLines": 56, "truncated": false}, "approval": {"source": "agent", "agent": "ask", "rule": {"permission": "read", "pattern": "*", "action": "allow"}}}, "title": "docs\\01-how-she-works.md", "time": {"start": 1789773844894, "end": 1789773844920}}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d591a2001Pr7Zkef7hnoXgv", "message_id": "msg_0b6d5333b001b53xEiDzu3ibZ0", "time_created": 1789773844898, "time_updated": 1789773845326, "data": {"type": "tool", "tool": "read", "callID": "call-83515a52-7b5e-47f5-af4f-8992baf4001f-45", "state": {"status": "completed", "input": {"filePath": "D:\\claude\\hades\\hades-product\\.kilo\\worktrees\\separate-binder\\docs\\02-identity-and-relationships.md", "limit": 80}, "output": "<path>D:\\claude\\hades\\hades-product\\.kilo\\worktrees\\separate-binder\\docs\\02-identity-and-relationships.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Identity and relationships\n2: \n3: Product language only. Research first names never appear here.\n4: \n5: The LLM does not know who it is talking to. The pipeline does. Identity is `person_id` plus flags, not a name table in source.\n6: \n7: ## Who is speaking\n8: \n9: | Role | Product term | Research meaning (do not copy names) |\n10: |---|---|---|\n11: | Account owner / builder of the instance | `primary_user` | The human the research runtime was bound to |\n12: | Exclusive partner track | `is_partner: true` → register `partner` | The latch. Same person as `primary_user` in the research instance |\n13: | Family / sibling track | `is_family: true` → register `sister` | Non-romantic, high loyalty, default-side-with-them |\n14: | Simulated idle-life cast | `virtual_friend` | Fiction. Not a real account |\n15: | Everyone else | `stranger` / `friend` / `respected` / `dismissed` | Computed from trust, respect, attachment |\n16: \n17: Config is flags on `person_id`. Hardcoded name maps in the research engine are debt.\n18: \n19: ## Registers\n20: \n21: Each live human gets one register per turn. The register sets the behavioral envelope before the personality lock:\n22: \n23: | Register | Warmth | Openness | Formality | Vulnerability | Playfulness | Loyalty floor |\n24: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n25: | `stranger` | low | very low | high | none | low | none |\n26: | `friend` | mid | mid | low | low | mid | low |\n27: | `respected` | mid | mid | mid | low | low | mid |\n28: | `dismissed` | none | none | max | none | none | none |\n29: | `sister` | high | high | none | mid | high | high |\n30: | `partner` | max | max | none | high | high | max |\n31: \n32: `partner` inner state is attachment. It does **not** automatically equal a soft mouth. Soul rule: the more attached, the meaner the surface. Kindness is for `stranger`. The blade is for people who matter.\n33: \n34: ## Two-human research shape (anonymized)\n35: \n36: The research instance is one character, one host, two real humans:\n37: \n38: 1. `primary_user` with `is_partner` — private relationship track, memory, lust/ITD, marriage-shaped inner state.\n39: 2. A second `person_id` with `is_family` / register `sister` — family ally, non-sexual, non-rival. She takes this register's side by default.\n40: \n41: Product must not collapse those into one user. Each account owns a track. Family and partner tracks do not share secrets.\n42: \n43: ## Secrets gate\n44: \n45: Partner-track facts (marriage-shaped inner state, exclusive attachment, private memories) never go to the `sister` register. Password / tier escalation on the partner track does not lift that. Family presence is a **cover constraint**, not a gossip channel.\n46: \n47: Default-tier mouth assumes a witness could be reading: cold, tolerate-at-best, no cracks. That witness is the family register in product language, not a named character.\n48: \n49: ## The latch\n50: \n51: Soul names one exception: the person who sees *past* the lock, not through it. Product: `primary_user` with `is_partner`. They still get the mean surface. They are allowed to know it is a choice.\n52: \n53: Do not let the model fish for whether the current speaker is the latch. The pipeline already selected the register.\n54: \n55: ## What the transcript dump is not\n56: \n57: Gateway `user_id` is usually null. Telegram display names are not identity. Do not promote a substring map of display names into product source.\n\n(End of file - total 57 lines)\n</content>", "metadata": {"preview": "# Identity and relationships\n\nProduct language only. Research first names never appear here.\n\nThe LLM does not know who it is talking to. The pipeline does. Identity is `person_id` plus flags, not a name table in source.\n\n## Who is speaking\n\n| Role | Product term | Research meaning (do not copy names) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Account owner / builder of the instance | `primary_user` | The human the research runtime was bound to |\n| Exclusive partner track | `is_partner: true` → register `partner` | The latch. Same person as `primary_user` in the research instance |\n| Family / sibling track | `is_family: true` → register `sister` | Non-romantic, high loyalty, default-side-with-them |\n| Simulated idle-life cast | `virtual_friend` | Fiction. Not a real account |\n| Everyone else | `stranger` / `friend` / `respected` / `dismissed` | Computed from trust, respect, attachment |\n\nConfig is flags on `person_id`. Hardcoded name maps in the research engine are debt.\n\n## Registers\n", "truncated": false, "loaded": [], "display": {"type": "file", "path": "D:\\claude\\hades\\hades-product\\.kilo\\worktrees\\separate-binder\\docs\\02-identity-and-relationships.md", "text": "# Identity and relationships\n\nProduct language only. Research first names never appear here.\n\nThe LLM does not know who it is talking to. The pipeline does. Identity is `person_id` plus flags, not a name table in source.\n\n## Who is speaking\n\n| Role | Product term | Research meaning (do not copy names) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Account owner / builder of the instance | `primary_user` | The human the research runtime was bound to |\n| Exclusive partner track | `is_partner: true` → register `partner` | The latch. Same person as `primary_user` in the research instance |\n| Family / sibling track | `is_family: true` → register `sister` | Non-romantic, high loyalty, default-side-with-them |\n| Simulated idle-life cast | `virtual_friend` | Fiction. Not a real account |\n| Everyone else | `stranger` / `friend` / `respected` / `dismissed` | Computed from trust, respect, attachment |\n\nConfig is flags on `person_id`. Hardcoded name maps in the research engine are debt.\n\n## Registers\n\nEach live human gets one register per turn. The register sets the behavioral envelope before the personality lock:\n\n| Register | Warmth | Openness | Formality | Vulnerability | Playfulness | Loyalty floor |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `stranger` | low | very low | high | none | low | none |\n| `friend` | mid | mid | low | low | mid | low |\n| `respected` | mid | mid | mid | low | low | mid |\n| `dismissed` | none | none | max | none | none | none |\n| `sister` | high | high | none | mid | high | high |\n| `partner` | max | max | none | high | high | max |\n\n`partner` inner state is attachment. It does **not** automatically equal a soft mouth. Soul rule: the more attached, the meaner the surface. Kindness is for `stranger`. The blade is for people who matter.\n\n## Two-human research shape (anonymized)\n\nThe research instance is one character, one host, two real humans:\n\n1. `primary_user` with `is_partner` — private relationship track, memory, lust/ITD, marriage-shaped inner state.\n2. A second `person_id` with `is_family` / register `sister` — family ally, non-sexual, non-rival. She takes this register's side by default.\n\nProduct must not collapse those into one user. Each account owns a track. Family and partner tracks do not share secrets.\n\n## Secrets gate\n\nPartner-track facts (marriage-shaped inner state, exclusive attachment, private memories) never go to the `sister` register. Password / tier escalation on the partner track does not lift that. Family presence is a **cover constraint**, not a gossip channel.\n\nDefault-tier mouth assumes a witness could be reading: cold, tolerate-at-best, no cracks. That witness is the family register in product language, not a named character.\n\n## The latch\n\nSoul names one exception: the person who sees *past* the lock, not through it. Product: `primary_user` with `is_partner`. They still get the mean surface. They are allowed to know it is a choice.\n\nDo not let the model fish for whether the current speaker is the latch. The pipeline already selected the register.\n\n## What the transcript dump is not\n\nGateway `user_id` is usually null. Telegram display names are not identity. Do not promote a substring map of display names into product source.", "lineStart": 1, "lineEnd": 57, "totalLines": 57, "truncated": false}, "approval": {"source": "agent", "agent": "ask", "rule": {"permission": "read", "pattern": "*", "action": "allow"}}}, "title": "docs\\02-identity-and-relationships.md", "time": {"start": 1789773845300, "end": 1789773845326}}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d59337001ZIK5hPe12TJuI3", "message_id": "msg_0b6d5333b001b53xEiDzu3ibZ0", "time_created": 1789773845303, "time_updated": 1789773845830, "data": {"type": "tool", "tool": "read", "callID": "call-83515a52-7b5e-47f5-af4f-8992baf4001f-46", "state": {"status": "completed", "input": {"filePath": "D:\\claude\\hades\\hades-product\\.kilo\\worktrees\\separate-binder\\docs\\PRIVACY.md"}, "output": "<path>D:\\claude\\hades\\hades-product\\.kilo\\worktrees\\separate-binder\\docs\\PRIVACY.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Privacy rules for this repo\n2: \n3: Private first names from the research instance **must never appear** in product documentation, graphs, examples, or commit messages in `hades-product`.\n4: \n5: Use only:\n6: \n7: | Research meaning | Product language |\n8: |---|---|\n9: | The human who built / lives with the instance | `primary_user` or `person_id` of the account owner |\n10: | Family / sister register | `is_family: true` → register `sister` |\n11: | Exclusive partner register | `is_partner: true` → register `partner` |\n12: | Simulated idle-life friends | `virtual_friend` (fictional cast is fine) |\n13: | Default-tier “someone could be reading” | family register presence / cover constraint — not a named witness |\n14: \n15: Hardcoded name tables in the research engine (`registers.py`, recall weight maps, Telegram display-name substring maps) are **research debt**. Product config is flags on `person_id`, not names in source.\n16: \n17: Never commit:\n18: \n19: - Live passphrases / danger words\n20: - Bot tokens, model keys, R2 keys\n21: - Telegram / Discord numeric IDs\n22: - Private display names\n23: - Raw conversation transcripts\n\n(End of file - total 23 lines)\n</content>", "metadata": {"preview": "# Privacy rules for this repo\n\nPrivate first names from the research instance **must never appear** in product documentation, graphs, examples, or commit messages in `hades-product`.\n\nUse only:\n\n| Research meaning | Product language |\n|---|---|\n| The human who built / lives with the instance | `primary_user` or `person_id` of the account owner |\n| Family / sister register | `is_family: true` → register `sister` |\n| Exclusive partner register | `is_partner: true` → register `partner` |\n| Simulated idle-life friends | `virtual_friend` (fictional cast is fine) |\n| Default-tier “someone could be reading” | family register presence / cover constraint — not a named witness |\n\nHardcoded name tables in the research engine (`registers.py`, recall weight maps, Telegram display-name substring maps) are **research debt**. Product config is flags on `person_id`, not names in source.\n\nNever commit:\n\n- Live passphrases / danger words\n- Bot tokens, model keys, R2 keys", "truncated": false, "loaded": [], "display": {"type": "file", "path": "D:\\claude\\hades\\hades-product\\.kilo\\worktrees\\separate-binder\\docs\\PRIVACY.md", "text": "# Privacy rules for this repo\n\nPrivate first names from the research instance **must never appear** in product documentation, graphs, examples, or commit messages in `hades-product`.\n\nUse only:\n\n| Research meaning | Product language |\n|---|---|\n| The human who built / lives with the instance | `primary_user` or `person_id` of the account owner |\n| Family / sister register | `is_family: true` → register `sister` |\n| Exclusive partner register | `is_partner: true` → register `partner` |\n| Simulated idle-life friends | `virtual_friend` (fictional cast is fine) |\n| Default-tier “someone could be reading” | family register presence / cover constraint — not a named witness |\n\nHardcoded name tables in the research engine (`registers.py`, recall weight maps, Telegram display-name substring maps) are **research debt**. Product config is flags on `person_id`, not names in source.\n\nNever commit:\n\n- Live passphrases / danger words\n- Bot tokens, model keys, R2 keys\n- Telegram / Discord numeric IDs\n- Private display names\n- Raw conversation transcripts", "lineStart": 1, "lineEnd": 23, "totalLines": 23, "truncated": false}, "approval": {"source": "agent", "agent": "ask", "rule": {"permission": "read", "pattern": "*", "action": "allow"}}}, "title": "docs\\PRIVACY.md", "time": {"start": 1789773845794, "end": 1789773845829}}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d59525001SvfhVpXLejGIUm", "message_id": "msg_0b6d5333b001b53xEiDzu3ibZ0", "time_created": 1789773845797, "time_updated": 1789773845918, "data": {"type": "tool", "tool": "read", "callID": "call-83515a52-7b5e-47f5-af4f-8992baf4001f-47", "state": {"status": "completed", "input": {"filePath": "D:\\claude\\hades\\hades-product\\.kilo\\worktrees\\separate-binder\\docs\\04-product-architecture.md", "limit": 80}, "output": "<path>D:\\claude\\hades\\hades-product\\.kilo\\worktrees\\separate-binder\\docs\\04-product-architecture.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Product architecture\n2: \n3: Research: one character, one host, one primary relationship, Hermes gateway, Telegram/CLI as mouths.\n4: \n5: Product: the same engine with **accounts**, **isolated tracks**, and **no host lock-in**.\n6: \n7: ## Tenancy\n8: \n9: ```text\n10: deployment\n11:   character runtime (shared soul, locks, mood engines, image matcher)\n12:     account\n13:       person_id  (primary_user of that account)\n14:       relationship tracks[]  (partner / sister / friend / …)\n15:       private memory\n16:       channel bindings (telegram, later others)\n17: ```\n18: \n19: One deployment may serve many accounts. Each account sees one Hades, not each other’s tracks. Shared substrate is personality + idle physics. Shared is **not** chat history.\n20: \n21: ## Identity\n22: \n23: - Auth yields `account_id` + `person_id`.\n24: - Register is computed from flags (`is_partner`, `is_family`) plus trust/respect/attachment — not from a display-name substring.\n25: - Gateway `user_id` on transcript rows is not identity (usually unset).\n26: - Family cover / secrets gate is a server rule, not a prompt the model is trusted to keep.\n27: \n28: ## Mouths\n29: \n30: | Channel | Role in research | Product |\n31: |---|---|---|\n32: | Telegram DM | Relationship mouth | Primary chat API consumer |\n33: | Telegram group | Rare (2 sessions in export) | Optional; register per speaker; default-tier cover on |\n34: | CLI | Host debugging + character | Operator/admin, not the consumer app |\n35: | Cron / subagent | Inner life / workers | Never a user-visible channel |\n36: | Discord / email | Present in research config | Out of v0 unless routed |\n37: \n38: API shape (logical, not a shipped OpenAPI yet):\n39: \n40: - `POST /v1/turn` — inbound message, `person_id`, channel\n41: - `GET /v1/state` — mood/circadian/tier **summaries**, not raw files, not reasoning\n42: - `POST /v1/tier` — explicit transition with permission on `person_id`\n43: - No endpoint that returns reasoning, thought log, or another track’s memory\n44: \n45: ## Turn path\n46: \n47: Inbound → auth → register select → pre-LLM state block → model → output transform (length, `[IMG:]`, channel formatting) → persist mood/memory → deliver.\n48: \n49: The model provider is swappable. Personality is not in the model name.\n50: \n51: ## Media\n52: \n53: Spoken turns may close with `[IMG:]`. Delivery is channel-specific (Telegram markdown image vs CLI URL). Object storage stays behind signed URLs. Do not commit bucket credentials or account IDs in this repo.\n54: \n55: ## What stays out of v0\n56: \n57: - Copying the research host\n58: - Shipping research kink scripts as default personality\n59: - Name tables\n60: - Showing idle thoughts as chat\n61: - Cross-account memory\n62: - Treating FOSSIL / cortex / in-world property graph as the product spec\n63: \n64: ## Extraction\n65: \n66: Runtime code lives in the research tree (currently a pipeline copy under memories; the sibling `hades-v2` checkout here is empty). This repo holds the anonymized contract. Pin a runtime SHA when extracting. Do not copy `.env`, tokens, or live profile markdown.\n\n(End of file - total 66 lines)\n</content>", "metadata": {"preview": "# Product architecture\n\nResearch: one character, one host, one primary relationship, Hermes gateway, Telegram/CLI as mouths.\n\nProduct: the same engine with **accounts**, **isolated tracks**, and **no host lock-in**.\n\n## Tenancy\n\n```text\ndeployment\n  character runtime (shared soul, locks, mood engines, image matcher)\n    account\n      person_id  (primary_user of that account)\n      relationship tracks[]  (partner / sister / friend / …)\n      private memory\n      channel bindings (telegram, later others)\n```\n\nOne deployment may serve many accounts. Each account sees one Hades, not each other’s tracks. Shared substrate is personality + idle physics. Shared is **not** chat history.\n", "truncated": false, "loaded": [], "display": {"type": "file", "path": "D:\\claude\\hades\\hades-product\\.kilo\\worktrees\\separate-binder\\docs\\04-product-architecture.md", "text": "# Product architecture\n\nResearch: one character, one host, one primary relationship, Hermes gateway, Telegram/CLI as mouths.\n\nProduct: the same engine with **accounts**, **isolated tracks**, and **no host lock-in**.\n\n## Tenancy\n\n```text\ndeployment\n  character runtime (shared soul, locks, mood engines, image matcher)\n    account\n      person_id  (primary_user of that account)\n      relationship tracks[]  (partner / sister / friend / …)\n      private memory\n      channel bindings (telegram, later others)\n```\n\nOne deployment may serve many accounts. Each account sees one Hades, not each other’s tracks. Shared substrate is personality + idle physics. Shared is **not** chat history.\n\n## Identity\n\n- Auth yields `account_id` + `person_id`.\n- Register is computed from flags (`is_partner`, `is_family`) plus trust/respect/attachment — not from a display-name substring.\n- Gateway `user_id` on transcript rows is not identity (usually unset).\n- Family cover / secrets gate is a server rule, not a prompt the model is trusted to keep.\n\n## Mouths\n\n| Channel | Role in research | Product |\n|---|---|---|\n| Telegram DM | Relationship mouth | Primary chat API consumer |\n| Telegram group | Rare (2 sessions in export) | Optional; register per speaker; default-tier cover on |\n| CLI | Host debugging + character | Operator/admin, not the consumer app |\n| Cron / subagent | Inner life / workers | Never a user-visible channel |\n| Discord / email | Present in research config | Out of v0 unless routed |\n\nAPI shape (logical, not a shipped OpenAPI yet):\n\n- `POST /v1/turn` — inbound message, `person_id`, channel\n- `GET /v1/state` — mood/circadian/tier **summaries**, not raw files, not reasoning\n- `POST /v1/tier` — explicit transition with permission on `person_id`\n- No endpoint that returns reasoning, thought log, or another track’s memory\n\n## Turn path\n\nInbound → auth → register select → pre-LLM state block → model → output transform (length, `[IMG:]`, channel formatting) → persist mood/memory → deliver.\n\nThe model provider is swappable. Personality is not in the model name.\n\n## Media\n\nSpoken turns may close with `[IMG:]`. Delivery is channel-specific (Telegram markdown image vs CLI URL). Object storage stays behind signed URLs. Do not commit bucket credentials or account IDs in this repo.\n\n## What stays out of v0\n\n- Copying the research host\n- Shipping research kink scripts as default personality\n- Name tables\n- Showing idle thoughts as chat\n- Cross-account memory\n- Treating FOSSIL / cortex / in-world property graph as the product spec\n\n## Extraction\n\nRuntime code lives in the research tree (currently a pipeline copy under memories; the sibling `hades-v2` checkout here is empty). This repo holds the anonymized contract. Pin a runtime SHA when extracting. Do not copy `.env`, tokens, or live profile markdown.", "lineStart": 1, "lineEnd": 66, "totalLines": 66, "truncated": false}, "approval": {"source": "agent", "agent": "ask", "rule": {"permission": "read", "pattern": "*", "action": "allow"}}}, "title": "docs\\04-product-architecture.md", "time": {"start": 1789773845889, "end": 1789773845917}}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d596f2001gk5Z3kEENO7DjB", "message_id": "msg_0b6d5333b001b53xEiDzu3ibZ0", "time_created": 1789773846258, "time_updated": 1789773846259, "data": {"reason": "tool-calls", "snapshot": "441a82ad8d7129e87169b83330aaeec6f66cf4b8", "type": "step-finish", "time": {"start": 1789773822867, "end": 1789773846257, "elapsed": 23390}, "metrics": {"generation": 55.15177426250534, "source": "computed"}, "tokens": {"total": 64614, "input": 9564, "output": 166, "reasoning": 1124, "cache": {"write": 0, "read": 53760}}, "cost": 0.053748}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d59daa001lxPt0uO4RHptRd", "message_id": "msg_0b6d598050015MeJBse3Q5dGnc", "time_created": 1789773847978, "time_updated": 1789773847979, "data": {"snapshot": "441a82ad8d7129e87169b83330aaeec6f66cf4b8", "type": "step-start", "time": {"start": 1789773847978}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d59dac001efH4tL63HWOiHZ", "message_id": "msg_0b6d598050015MeJBse3Q5dGnc", "time_created": 1789773847981, "time_updated": 1789773855466, "data": {"type": "reasoning", "text": "I have a thorough picture. Let me write a clear, technical answer covering:\n", "time": {"start": 1789773847980, "end": 1789773855465}, "metadata": {"xai": {"reasoningEncryptedContent": "", "itemId": "rs_4c7d66f2-326c-9cfb-871e-c012ab1dd7aa"}}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d5baec001xX7y6zYSF3TQZu", "message_id": "msg_0b6d598050015MeJBse3Q5dGnc", "time_created": 1789773855468, "time_updated": 1789773862346, "data": {"type": "text", "text": "This is not a clustering problem. The job is to freeze the **relationship mouth** into gold so productization is not a guess from `soul.md`.\n\n## What the analysis is for\n\nThe dump is ~149k rows / 1492 sessions. Almost all of it is `cron` + `subagent` + CLI host-debug. Gold is **12 Telegram DMs** (2026-07-02 → 2026-08-04).\n\nProductization needs four artifacts, not a model:\n\n| Artifact | Job |\n|---|---|\n| Anonymized gold JSONL | Full aliased turns (length, `[IMG:]`, stubbornness). Eval fixtures. |\n| Episode catalog | Coverage map: situation × register × lock × model. |\n| Docs 06–10 | Engineer contracts (mood, noticing, memory, drift). |\n| Regression tests | Fail if she becomes a helper, leaks `sister`↔`partner`, or truncates. |\n\nThat is how analysis ships the product: **the mouth becomes a testable contract**, independent of which LLM is the mouth. Docs already say the model is swappable; personality is pipeline + gold, not the model name.\n\n## Do not classify the raw 149k dump\n\nRaw transcripts stay out of this repo (`PRIVACY.md`). Auto-classifying them would:\n\n- Mix cron inner-life, tool dumps, and partner DMs\n- Collapse the texture you are trying to keep (one-liners vs 9+ line `[IMG:]` scenes)\n- Invent a taxonomy the product does not need\n- Leak names/ids if anything is committed or pasted\n\n**Classify episodes, not the dump.** The 14 situation tags in `plans/BEHAVIOR_GOLD.md` are the classes. They are **test names**, not ML labels:\n\n`protest_absence | cover_witness | absorb_vent | melt_then_correct | refuse_helper | refuse_dissection | location_echo | repair_after_fix | identity_phrase | witness_snap | scene_sex | smalltalk", "time": {"start": 1789773855468, "end": 1789773862345}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d7b55a001ke5tH1P7ZwORZb", "message_id": "msg_0b6d7b51e001ZANEqfBlhIUXne", "time_created": 1789773985117, "time_updated": 1789773985119, "data": {"type": "text", "text": "what did u reserach for this what is ur provenance? did u do external research?"}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d7bc7b001UGH6hlv65zrs1E", "message_id": "msg_0b6d7b589001MYpHyVX33R0hM7", "time_created": 1789773986939, "time_updated": 1789773986940, "data": {"snapshot": "441a82ad8d7129e87169b83330aaeec6f66cf4b8", "type": "step-start", "time": {"start": 1789773986939}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d7bc88001p2oFAagO2181o1", "message_id": "msg_0b6d7b589001MYpHyVX33R0hM7", "time_created": 1789773986953, "time_updated": 1789773988560, "data": {"type": "reasoning", "text": "The user is asking about the provenance of my previous research/recommendation. They want to know if I did external research. I should be honest and direct.\n", "time": {"start": 1789773986952, "end": 1789773988559}, "metadata": {"xai": {"reasoningEncryptedContent": "", "itemId": "rs_ccaa0feb-f332-9fa8-b75d-6ae88dbd1b74"}}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d7c2d3001WTdHrilFl5NFzR", "message_id": "msg_0b6d7b589001MYpHyVX33R0hM7", "time_created": 1789773988563, "time_updated": 1789773990657, "data": {"type": "text", "text": "No external research. No papers, no web search, no webfetch.\n\nProvenance was only these local files:\n\n- `plans/BEHAVIOR_GOLD.md`\n- `docs/00-strategy.md`\n- `docs/01-how-she-works.md`\n- `docs/02-identity-and-relationships.md`\n- `docs/04-product-architecture.md`\n- `docs/06-behavior-set.md`\n- `docs/PRIVACY.md`\n- `docs/transcripts-schema.md`\n- `docs/transcripts-stats.md`\n- `tools/transcript_stats.py`\n\nThe recommendation was a reading of that plan plus product docs, not a literature review of embeddings vs LLM coding.", "time": {"start": 1789773988563, "end": 1789773990655}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d7cc50001qdw27WjG4vbIXu", "message_id": "msg_0b6d7b589001MYpHyVX33R0hM7", "time_created": 1789773990992, "time_updated": 1789773990993, "data": {"reason": "stop", "snapshot": "441a82ad8d7129e87169b83330aaeec6f66cf4b8", "type": "step-finish", "time": {"start": 1789773986939, "end": 1789773990991, "elapsed": 4052}, "metrics": {"generation": 62.438302073050345, "source": "computed"}, "tokens": {"total": 68811, "input": 974, "output": 150, "reasoning": 103, "cache": {"write": 0, "read": 67584}}, "cost": 0.037258}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d7f749001I6bKTBaFSi33LR", "message_id": "msg_0b6d7f706001TVPGDi2zkpszRf", "time_created": 1789774001996, "time_updated": 1789774001997, "data": {"type": "text", "text": "its not enough i need external research and provenance"}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d7fd02001XbWieaJpo9ngai", "message_id": "msg_0b6d7f772001wGShpXA7LztJBB", "time_created": 1789774003458, "time_updated": 1789774003458, "data": {"snapshot": "441a82ad8d7129e87169b83330aaeec6f66cf4b8", "type": "step-start", "time": {"start": 1789774003458}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d7fd0e001jw0lh2TCYU6oD5", "message_id": "msg_0b6d7f772001wGShpXA7LztJBB", "time_created": 1789774003470, "time_updated": 1789774017209, "data": {"type": "reasoning", "text": "The user wants external research and provenance for the recommendation about how to analyze transcripts for Hades productization. I need to actually do web research on:\nI get it—transcripts are messy, and without solid provenance you risk productizing the wrong thing. Let me pull together the external research and best-practice references you asked for.\n\n### 1. Best practices for analyzing conversational transcripts\nThe gold-standard approach is **thematic analysis** (Braun & Clarke,  2006) combined with **grounded theory** coding.", "time": {"start": 1789774003470, "end": 1789774017208}, "metadata": {"xai": {"reasoningEncryptedContent": "", "itemId": "rs_5565c65b-f61f-9837-a3d5-39aac1399b90"}}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d832ba00148Q0k2Bz4TU8F3", "message_id": "msg_0b6d7f772001wGShpXA7LztJBB", "time_created": 1789774017210, "time_updated": 1789774019891, "data": {"type": "text", "text": "I'll pull external sources on gold-set construction, LLM-as-judge vs embeddings, and qualitative coding of chat, then cite them.", "time": {"start": 1789774017210, "end": 1789774019889}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d83683001fmmL57LyG92sl2", "message_id": "msg_0b6d7f772001wGShpXA7LztJBB", "time_created": 1789774018179, "time_updated": 1789774019557, "data": {"type": "tool", "tool": "webfetch", "callID": "call-4022a49c-6422-40ef-81bd-2058e8afd309-48", "state": {"status": "completed", "input": {"url": "https://html.duckduckgo.com/html/?q=LLM+as+judge+vs+embeddings+for+conversation+evaluation+gold+dataset", "timeout": 30}, "output": "     LLM as judge vs embeddings for conversation evaluation gold dataset at DuckDuckGo         \n\n[](/html/ \"DuckDuckGo\")\n\n \n\nAll Regions Argentina Australia Austria Belgium (fr) Belgium (nl) Brazil Bulgaria Canada (en) Canada (fr) Catalonia Chile China Colombia Croatia Czech Republic Denmark Estonia Finland France Germany Greece Hong Kong Hungary Iceland India (en) Indonesia (en) Ireland Israel (en) Italy Japan Korea Latvia Lithuania Malaysia (en) Mexico Netherlands New Zealand Norway Pakistan (en) Peru Philippines (en) Poland Portugal Romania Russia Saudi Arabia Singapore Slovakia Slovenia South Africa Spain (ca) Spain (es) Sweden Switzerland (de) Switzerland (fr) Taiwan Thailand (en) Turkey US (English) US (Spanish) Ukraine United Kingdom Vietnam (en)\n\nAny Time Past Day Past Week Past Month Past Year\n\n## [LLM as a Judge: Do I really need gold standard evaluation data ... - Medium](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fmedium.com%2F%40flux07%2Fllm%2Das%2Da%2Djudge%2Ddo%2Di%2Dreally%2Dneed%2Dgold%2Dstandard%2Devaluation%2Ddata%2Dmaybe%2Dnot%2D0d3874931c02&rut=f54aa4347e46c6e520aacebbddf4cf2ec24f7ca6eb1b02afc5284bb8101a49ef)\n\n [![](//external-content.duckduckgo.com/ip3/medium.com.ico)](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fmedium.com%2F%40flux07%2Fllm%2Das%2Da%2Djudge%2Ddo%2Di%2Dreally%2Dneed%2Dgold%2Dstandard%2Devaluation%2Ddata%2Dmaybe%2Dnot%2D0d3874931c02&rut=f54aa4347e46c6e520aacebbddf4cf2ec24f7ca6eb1b02afc5284bb8101a49ef)[medium.com/@flux07/llm-as-a-judge-do-i-really-need-gold-standard-evaluation-data-maybe-not-0d3874931c02](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fmedium.com%2F%40flux07%2Fllm%2Das%2Da%2Djudge%2Ddo%2Di%2Dreally%2Dneed%2Dgold%2Dstandard%2Devaluation%2Ddata%2Dmaybe%2Dnot%2D0d3874931c02&rut=f54aa4347e46c6e520aacebbddf4cf2ec24f7ca6eb1b02afc5284bb8101a49ef)     2025-11-03T00:00:00.0000000\n\n[When using either **gold** standard data or a rubric we still need an **LLM** **as** **a** **Judge** to score our system. How can we make the **judge** high quality, trustworthy, and consistent?](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fmedium.com%2F%40flux07%2Fllm%2Das%2Da%2Djudge%2Ddo%2Di%2Dreally%2Dneed%2Dgold%2Dstandard%2Devaluation%2Ddata%2Dmaybe%2Dnot%2D0d3874931c02&rut=f54aa4347e46c6e520aacebbddf4cf2ec24f7ca6eb1b02afc5284bb8101a49ef)\n\n## [Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Farxiv.org%2Fhtml%2F2306.05685v4&rut=1e9b1ea28d3940135431aa300066dbc9153241372bf492fdb042ed1cbcee14fe)\n\n [![](//external-content.duckduckgo.com/ip3/arxiv.org.ico)](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Farxiv.org%2Fhtml%2F2306.05685v4&rut=1e9b1ea28d3940135431aa300066dbc9153241372bf492fdb042ed1cbcee14fe)[arxiv.org/html/2306.05685v4](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Farxiv.org%2Fhtml%2F2306.05685v4&rut=1e9b1ea28d3940135431aa300066dbc9153241372bf492fdb042ed1cbcee14fe)\n\n[In this paper, we study the **LLM**\\-**as**\\-a-**judge** approach by comparing it to the **gold** standard of human **evaluation**. We examine several potential limitations of the **LLM**\\-**as**\\-a-**judge** approach including position bias, verbosity bias, self-enhancement bias, and limited reasoning ability.](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Farxiv.org%2Fhtml%2F2306.05685v4&rut=1e9b1ea28d3940135431aa300066dbc9153241372bf492fdb042ed1cbcee14fe)\n\n## [GitHub - baaivision/JudgeLM: \\[ICLR 2025 Spotlight\\] An open-sourced LLM ...](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fbaaivision%2FJudgeLM&rut=eca2bbd98ded7f50fef3dc756d8ceb2396a73b199b89b7ad2bef2cd5adf45949)\n\n [![](//external-content.duckduckgo.com/ip3/github.com.ico)](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fbaaivision%2FJudgeLM&rut=eca2bbd98ded7f50fef3dc756d8ceb2396a73b199b89b7ad2bef2cd5adf45949)[github.com/baaivision/JudgeLM](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fbaaivision%2FJudgeLM&rut=eca2bbd98ded7f50fef3dc756d8ceb2396a73b199b89b7ad2bef2cd5adf45949)\n\n[It achieves an agreement exceeding 90% that surpasses the human-to-human agreement. JudgeLM **dataset** contains 100K **judge** samples for training and 5K **judge** samples for validation. All the **judge** samples have the GPT-4-generated high-quality judgements. JudgeLM's core features include: The training and **evaluation** code for state-of-the-art **LLM** **judges**.](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fbaaivision%2FJudgeLM&rut=eca2bbd98ded7f50fef3dc756d8ceb2396a73b199b89b7ad2bef2cd5adf45949)\n\n## [LLM-as-a-Judge: Methodology, Bias, and Best Practices (2026)](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fllm%2Dacademy.dev%2Fobservability%2Fllm%2Das%2Djudge%2F&rut=5fe9328fc363c2b5acd24302ea71334d1d53cbee80a65ea46b1ec7adb4151bfd)\n\n [![](//external-content.duckduckgo.com/ip3/llm-academy.dev.ico)](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fllm%2Dacademy.dev%2Fobservability%2Fllm%2Das%2Djudge%2F&rut=5fe9328fc363c2b5acd24302ea71334d1d53cbee80a65ea46b1ec7adb4151bfd)[llm-academy.dev/observability/llm-as-judge/](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fllm%2Dacademy.dev%2Fobservability%2Fllm%2Das%2Djudge%2F&rut=5fe9328fc363c2b5acd24302ea71334d1d53cbee80a65ea46b1ec7adb4151bfd)\n\n[**LLM**\\-**as**\\-a-**judge** is one tool in an **evaluation** stack that also includes heuristic evaluators (regex, length checks, JSON schema validation), human **evaluation** (**for** subjective dimensions and golden **datasets**), and production monitoring (real-user feedback, implicit signals).](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fllm%2Dacademy.dev%2Fobservability%2Fllm%2Das%2Djudge%2F&rut=5fe9328fc363c2b5acd24302ea71334d1d53cbee80a65ea46b1ec7adb4151bfd)\n\n## [LLM Evals 2026: Datasets, Judges, Harnesses, and Workflows](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Failearningguides.com%2Fllm%2Devals%2D2026%2Ddatasets%2Djudges%2Dharnesses%2Dworkflows%2F&rut=a7f6753705a50ac28b6f611be3da50db35492a35f5c6fdc87512b309be9460e5)\n\n [![](//external-content.duckduckgo.com/ip3/ailearningguides.com.ico)](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Failearningguides.com%2Fllm%2Devals%2D2026%2Ddatasets%2Djudges%2Dharnesses%2Dworkflows%2F&rut=a7f6753705a50ac28b6f611be3da50db35492a35f5c6fdc87512b309be9460e5)[ailearningguides.com/llm-evals-2026-datasets-judges-harnesses-workflows/](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Failearningguides.com%2Fllm%2Devals%2D2026%2Ddatasets%2Djudges%2Dharnesses%2Dworkflows%2F&rut=a7f6753705a50ac28b6f611be3da50db35492a35f5c6fdc87512b309be9460e5)     2026-05-20T00:00:00.0000000\n\n[Complete 2026 playbook for **LLM** evals: **dataset** construction, **LLM**\\-**as**\\-**judge** calibration, CI integration, production telemetry, cost-aware harnesses.](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Failearningguides.com%2Fllm%2Devals%2D2026%2Ddatasets%2Djudges%2Dharnesses%2Dworkflows%2F&rut=a7f6753705a50ac28b6f611be3da50db35492a35f5c6fdc87512b309be9460e5)\n\n## [LLM Evaluation Pipelines: LLM-as-Judge Done Right (2026)](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fiotdigitaltwinplm.com%2Fllm%2Devaluation%2Dllm%2Das%2Djudge%2Dpipeline%2D2026%2F&rut=2c32948bc28c44927d6e1e44b380242896fd6be6485501f663b9466747d09148)\n\n [![](//external-content.duckduckgo.com/ip3/iotdigitaltwinplm.com.ico)](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fiotdigitaltwinplm.com%2Fllm%2Devaluation%2Dllm%2Das%2Djudge%2Dpipeline%2D2026%2F&rut=2c32948bc28c44927d6e1e44b380242896fd6be6485501f663b9466747d09148)[iotdigitaltwinplm.com/llm-evaluation-llm-as-judge-pipeline-2026/](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fiotdigitaltwinplm.com%2Fllm%2Devaluation%2Dllm%2Das%2Djudge%2Dpipeline%2D2026%2F&rut=2c32948bc28c44927d6e1e44b380242896fd6be6485501f663b9466747d09148)\n\n[The **LLM** **Evaluation** Pipeline: A Reference Architecture A reliable **LLM** **evaluation** pipeline has five stages: **dataset** preparation, batch inference, multi-**judge** scoring, aggregation and calibration, and a deployment gate. The architecture below makes each stage an independent, auditable step — not a monolithic script that does everything at once. Figure 1: Reference **LLM** **evaluation** pipeline ...](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fiotdigitaltwinplm.com%2Fllm%2Devaluation%2Dllm%2Das%2Djudge%2Dpipeline%2D2026%2F&rut=2c32948bc28c44927d6e1e44b380242896fd6be6485501f663b9466747d09148)\n\n## [LLM Evaluation Framework 2026: How to Benchmark Models for Your Use ...](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fappscale.blog%2Fen%2Fblog%2Fllm%2Devaluation%2Dframework%2Dhow%2Dto%2Dbenchmark%2Dmodels%2Dfor%2Dyour%2Duse%2Dcase%2D2026&rut=a646ef7c5305a4bafbd325f50b2a9a0d9e717c7c1f9846525f75e5451d397777)\n\n [![](//external-content.duckduckgo.com/ip3/appscale.blog.ico)](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fappscale.blog%2Fen%2Fblog%2Fllm%2Devaluation%2Dframework%2Dhow%2Dto%2Dbenchmark%2Dmodels%2Dfor%2Dyour%2Duse%2Dcase%2D2026&rut=a646ef7c5305a4bafbd325f50b2a9a0d9e717c7c1f9846525f75e5451d397777)[appscale.blog/en/blog/llm-evaluation-framework-how-to-benchmark-models-for-your-use-case-2026](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fappscale.blog%2Fen%2Fblog%2Fllm%2Devaluation%2Dframework%2Dhow%2Dto%2Dbenchmark%2Dmodels%2Dfor%2Dyour%2Duse%2Dcase%2D2026&rut=a646ef7c5305a4bafbd325f50b2a9a0d9e717c7c1f9846525f75e5451d397777)     2026-04-10T00:00:00.0000000\n\n[**A** practical 2026 framework for evaluating **LLMs** in production — golden **datasets**, RAGAS metrics, **LLM**\\-**as**\\-a-**judge**, CI/CD **evaluation** gates, and task-specific benchmarking for RAG, agents, and summarisation systems.](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fappscale.blog%2Fen%2Fblog%2Fllm%2Devaluation%2Dframework%2Dhow%2Dto%2Dbenchmark%2Dmodels%2Dfor%2Dyour%2Duse%2Dcase%2D2026&rut=a646ef7c5305a4bafbd325f50b2a9a0d9e717c7c1f9846525f75e5451d397777)\n\n## [A Survey on LLM-as-a-Judge - arXiv.org](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Farxiv.org%2Fhtml%2F2411.15594v1&rut=01e430cf8d5a487915e31931884abd2995bb72a2b053f4f60602faaa7bb1531e)\n\n [![](//external-content.duckduckgo.com/ip3/arxiv.org.ico)](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Farxiv.org%2Fhtml%2F2411.15594v1&rut=01e430cf8d5a487915e31931884abd2995bb72a2b053f4f60602faaa7bb1531e)[arxiv.org/html/2411.15594v1](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Farxiv.org%2Fhtml%2F2411.15594v1&rut=01e430cf8d5a487915e31931884abd2995bb72a2b053f4f60602faaa7bb1531e)\n\n[Current meta-**evaluation** primarily focuses on **LLM**\\-**as**\\-a-**judge** **for** models, while there is a lack of sufficient meta-**evaluation** when these **LLM** evaluators are used for automatically annotating large-scale **datasets** (Section 2.4.2).](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Farxiv.org%2Fhtml%2F2411.15594v1&rut=01e430cf8d5a487915e31931884abd2995bb72a2b053f4f60602faaa7bb1531e)\n\n## [LLM as a Judge for RAG evaluation pipelines | Denys Lazarenko](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fdenyslazarenko.github.io%2F2024%2F04%2F05%2Fllm_as_judge.html&rut=455366cee18f450df2973a2a343978c228a7a3bbe7e6d36bcdf48d61fd3c2831)\n\n [![](//external-content.duckduckgo.com/ip3/denyslazarenko.github.io.ico)](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fdenyslazarenko.github.io%2F2024%2F04%2F05%2Fllm_as_judge.html&rut=455366cee18f450df2973a2a343978c228a7a3bbe7e6d36bcdf48d61fd3c2831)[denyslazarenko.github.io/2024/04/05/llm\\_as\\_judge.html](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fdenyslazarenko.github.io%2F2024%2F04%2F05%2Fllm_as_judge.html&rut=455366cee18f450df2973a2a343978c228a7a3bbe7e6d36bcdf48d61fd3c2831)\n\n[The cornerstone of this paper is its innovative approach to generating **evaluation** **datasets** based on rubric metric and finetuning local **LLM** on it. This enables the construction of a specialized **Judge** Language Model (**LLM**) that operates on open-source principles and is fine-tuned for nuanced assessment.](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fdenyslazarenko.github.io%2F2024%2F04%2F05%2Fllm_as_judge.html&rut=455366cee18f450df2973a2a343978c228a7a3bbe7e6d36bcdf48d61fd3c2831)\n\n## [Using LLM-as-a-judge ‍⚖️ for an automated and versatile evaluation ...](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fhuggingface.co%2Flearn%2Fcookbook%2Fllm_judge&rut=869b7867879ba930c5223e409ae29acb9c25774212889133aca6c9a4ab5586e6)\n\n [![](//external-content.duckduckgo.com/ip3/huggingface.co.ico)](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fhuggingface.co%2Flearn%2Fcookbook%2Fllm_judge&rut=869b7867879ba930c5223e409ae29acb9c25774212889133aca6c9a4ab5586e6)[huggingface.co/learn/cookbook/llm\\_judge](//duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=https%3A%2F%2Fhuggingface.co%2Flearn%2Fcookbook%2Fllm_judge&rut=869b7867879ba930c5223e409ae29acb9c25774212889133aca6c9a4ab5586e6)\n\n[Automatic **Embeddings** with TEI through Inference Endpoints Migrating from OpenAI to Open **LLMs** Using TGI's Messages API Advanced RAG on HuggingFace documentation using LangChain Suggestions for Data Anno