pdf (application/pdf)", "time": {"start": 1789774044251, "end": 1789774044516}}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d89f56001HFh6Qvp0D6hRVy", "message_id": "msg_0b6d85b980015beiZyYPYxA9sO", "time_created": 1789774045014, "time_updated": 1789774045014, "data": {"reason": "tool-calls", "snapshot": "441a82ad8d7129e87169b83330aaeec6f66cf4b8", "type": "step-finish", "time": {"start": 1789774039575, "end": 1789774045012, "elapsed": 5437}, "metrics": {"generation": 62.1666360125069, "source": "computed"}, "tokens": {"total": 146645, "input": 64259, "output": 108, "reasoning": 230, "cache": {"write": 0, "read": 82048}}, "cost": 0.17157}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d8c48e001sT7Xl88GDOB6ch", "message_id": "msg_0b6d8a072001kaVvpXc1PU7bV2", "time_created": 1789774054542, "time_updated": 1789774054543, "data": {"snapshot": "441a82ad8d7129e87169b83330aaeec6f66cf4b8", "type": "step-start", "time": {"start": 1789774054542}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d8c49a001whdQlpwFD0YS48", "message_id": "msg_0b6d8a072001kaVvpXc1PU7bV2", "time_created": 1789774054554, "time_updated": 1789774064890, "data": {"type": "reasoning", "text": "I have enough primary sources now. Let me also grab the Interactive intervention results from Hila & Hauser - they said Step-by-Step achieved accuracy 0.775, κ 0.744, α 0.746.\nI’ve got the external research locked in now—Hila & Hauser’s step-by-step protocol hitting κ 0.", "time": {"start": 1789774054554, "end": 1789774064889}, "metadata": {"xai": {"reasoningEncryptedContent": "", "itemId": "rs_2bfb3720-0ca4-944b-b166-235b7bf24c2c"}}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d8ecfc001DnTJgHADigb40N", "message_id": "msg_0b6d8a072001kaVvpXc1PU7bV2", "time_created": 1789774064892, "time_updated": 1789774092206, "data": {"type": "text", "text": "Provenance first, then what it implies. This pass is **external literature**, not a re-read of `plans/BEHAVIOR_GOLD.md`.\n\n## What I actually did\n\n| Step | What |\n|---|---|\n| Search | DuckDuckGo HTML: LLM-as-judge vs gold; LLM qualitative coding 2024–25; SBERT clustering (2 of 4 queries captcha’d) |\n| Fetched | Primary papers/docs below, not blog recaps |\n| Not done | No Character.AI / persona-consistency paper (gap). No BERTopic paper. No OpenAI evals cookbook. |\n\n## Primary sources\n\n**1. Zheng et al., 2023.** *Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena.* [arXiv:2306.05685](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05685)\n\n- Human preference is the gold; BLEU/ROUGE fail on open-ended chat.\n- Strong LLM judges ≈ human–human agreement (~80%+ on non-tie votes).\n- Biases: **position, verbosity, self-enhancement**. Verbosity attack: GPT-3.5/Claude fail ~91%; GPT-4 ~9%.\n- Fix: swap order, CoT, **reference-guided** grading (math fail 14/20 → 3/20).\n- Hybrid: capability benchmarks + preference gold, not one or the other.\n\n**2. Gu et al., 2024.** *A Survey on LLM-as-a-Judge.* [arXiv:2411.15594](https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.15594)\n\n- Human eval is still gold; slow/costly. LLM-as-judge is a **proxy**, not a replacement.\n- Pipeline: prompt (score / yes-no / pairwise / MC) → model choice → parse.\n- Pairwise aligns with humans better than absolute scores (they cite Liu et al. 2024).\n- Length/position/concreteness bias is the reliability problem.\n- **LLM-as-judge for data annotation** is a separate, under-meta-evaluated use (their §2.4.2 / §8.2). That is our catalog-labeling case.\n\n**3. Liu et al., 2023.** *G-Eval.* [arXiv:2303.16634](https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.16634)\n\n- GPT-4 + CoT + form-fill: Spearman **0.514** vs humans on summarization, better than BLEU/ROUGE.\n- LLM judges **prefer LLM-written text**. Bad if gold is “keep her real mouth, do not rewrite.”\n\n**4. Hugging Face cookbook (Roucher), citing Zheng.** [huggingface.co/learn/cookbook/llm_judge](https://huggingface.co/learn/cookbook/en/llm_judge)\n\n- Naive 0–10 float judge: Pearson **0.567** vs humans (human–human was 0.563).\n- Better prompt (1–4 integer, rationale first, scale text): **0.843**.\n- You still need a **small human calibration set** (~30) to trust the judge. 100% agreement is impossible because human gold is noisy.\n\n**5. Hila & Hauser, 2025.** *Assessing the Reliability of LLMs for Deductive Qualitative Coding.* [arXiv:2507.14384](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.14384)\n\nThis is the closest analog: **apply a pre-existing codebook** (not invent clusters).\n\n| Method | Accuracy | Cohen’s κ |\n|---|---|---|\n| ChatGPT zero-shot (no scaffold) | 0.57 | **0.46** (not reliable) |\n| BiLSTM | 0.59 | 0.55 |\n| RoBERTa (supervised on labeled data) | 0.79 | **0.75** |\n| ChatGPT **step-by-step + codebook** | **0.775** | **0.744** (substantial; Landis & Koch 0.61–0.80) |\n\n- Prompt strategy **changes the label distribution** (Cramér’s V up to **0.613** definitions vs few-shot). Same model, different scaffold = different taxonomy.\n- They cite Marathe & Toyama: researchers want automation **after** codebook + a human-coded subset, not instead of it.\n- Kappa 0.61–0.80 = substantial; Krippendorff α ≥ 0.80 = reliable, 0.667–0.80 = tentative.\n\n**6. Reimers & Gurevych, 2019.** *Sentence-BERT.* [arXiv:1908.10084](https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.10084)\n\n- SBERT is for **STS, clustering, retrieval** (cosine). BERT cross-encoder is for pair comparison; 10k sentences ≈ 50M pair inferences.\n- **Argument Facet Similarity** (claim + reasoning, not topical paraphrase): unsupervised GloVe/InferSent fail; even **task-trained SBERT drops ~7 Spearman points cross-topic** vs BERT cross-encoder.\n- That is the Hades failure mode: `protest_absence` vs `absorb_vent` is **situation + register**, not “similar words.”\n\n## Mapping onto Hades (external claims only)\n\n| Tool | External verdict | Hades use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Raw Grok on 149k rows | Zheng/Gu: judge without rubric + gold is uncalibrated; verbosity bias **rewards the long helper mouth you want to fail** | Do not bulk-classify the dump |\n| Deductive LLM coding (Grok + your 14 situations) | Hila: zero-shot κ=0.46; step-by-step+codebook κ≈0.74 | After a **human-labeled seed**, Grok can propose episode labels. Human still gates gold. |\n| Supervised classifier / RoBERTa | Beats unprompted ChatGPT **once labels exist** (κ=0.75) | Optional **later**, to find more candidates of a known situation. Not step 1. |\n| SBERT / embeddings | Built for similarity search, not argumentative coding; AFS shows topic ≠ situation | Optional **retrieval**: “find more like this gold episode.” Not the gold itself. |\n| LLM-as-judge of new mouths | Zheng ~80% human agree **if** rubric + reference; G-Eval: judges prefer LLM prose | Use **reference-guided** pairwise: new mouth vs **full aliased gold turn**, not a 1–10 “character score.” |\n\n## What this does *not* prove\n\n- No paper I fetched is about companion/character productization or 33-day Telegram DMs.\n- No result says “never use embeddings.” They are a **retriever**, not a spec.\n- Hila’s κ=0.74 is on **policy topics**, not `cover_witness` vs `melt_then_correct`. Expect worse until the codebook is tight.\n\n**Bottom line from this literature, not from the repo:** gold is a **small human-coded episode set**; Grok is a **deductive coder / judge against that set** after calibration; embeddings/classifiers are **scale tools after gold exists**. Using Grok or SBERT as the analysis *is* the product would import verbosity bias and topical clusters in place of the mouth contract.", "time": {"start": 1789774064892, "end": 1789774092205}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6d959f5001yI3XwDMs8C8CYc", "message_id": "msg_0b6d8a072001kaVvpXc1PU7bV2", "time_created": 1789774092789, "time_updated": 1789774092789, "data": {"reason": "stop", "snapshot": "441a82ad8d7129e87169b83330aaeec6f66cf4b8", "type": "step-finish", "time": {"start": 1789774054542, "end": 1789774092788, "elapsed": 38246}, "metrics": {"generation": 56.45034774878419, "source": "computed"}, "tokens": {"total": 187455, "input": 38992, "output": 1597, "reasoning": 562, "cache": {"write": 0, "read": 146304}}, "cost": 0.16409}}, {"id": "prt_0b6da08b4001zMXWefVXGapQUe", "message_id": "msg_0b6da0869001f6E040y2aCER3M", "time_created": 1789774137527, "time_updated": 1789774137528, "data": {"type": "text", "text": "how does it relate to our goal of analyzing how the character interaction actually work? also no its not just 33 day what we need, we need it to do it for atleast 3-4 month of her data 33 was jsut a quick example to create a plan"}}, {"id": "prt_0b6da1209001lSsxhBaAzclZrs", "message_id": "msg_0b6da08f90012KFdz97MLsVla7", "time_created": 1789774139914, "time_updated": 1789774139914, "data": {"snapshot": "441a82ad8d7129e87169b83330aaeec6f66cf4b8", "type": "step-start", "time": {"start": 1789774139913}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6da120d001B0WibPiA6Cy50S", "message_id": "msg_0b6da08f90012KFdz97MLsVla7", "time_created": 1789774139917, "time_updated": 1789774158305, "data": {"type": "reasoning", "text": "The user wants me to relate the research findings to their actual goal: analyzing how the character interaction actually works. They're also correcting me that 33 days was just a quick example for the...The research methods we’ve been developing—particularly the behavioral coding framework and the interaction loop analysis—map directly onto your goal of understanding how character interactions actually function. The 33-day window was just a proof-of-concept seed to test the coding scheme; the real analysis corpus needs to be 3–4 months of Telegram 1:1 DMs so we can see how the full causal chain (inbound → person/register → idle-state pre-LLM packet → LLM mouth → human reaction → writeback) evolves over time.", "time": {"start": 1789774139917, "end": 1789774158302}, "metadata": {"xai": {"reasoningEncryptedContent": "", "itemId": "rs_882de37f-8dbf-9c3f-bd7b-b589b5abb623"}}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6da59e50010ITI68q5KbNO32", "message_id": "msg_0b6da08f90012KFdz97MLsVla7", "time_created": 1789774158309, "time_updated": 1789774160864, "data": {"type": "text", "text": "33 days was a codebook seed, not the corpus. I'll map the literature onto “how the interaction actually works,” and check how much data we actually have versus 3–4 months.", "time": {"start": 1789774158309, "end": 1789774160863}}}, {"id": "prt_0b6da5eff001k2crIKOU5ZzPN4", "message_id": "msg_0b6da08f90012KFdz97MLsVla7", "time_created": 1789774159615, "time_updated": 1789774160252, "data": {"type": "tool", "tool": "read", "callID": "call-b0d15dcd-3e2a-4e37-909d-c84aecffeebd-60", "state": {"status": "completed", "input": {"filePath": "D:\\claude\\hades\\hades-product\\.kilo\\worktrees\\separate-binder\\docs\\transcripts-stats.md", "offset": 1, "limit": 25}, "output": "<path>D:\\claude\\hades\\hades-product\\.kilo\\worktrees\\separate-binder\\docs\\transcripts-stats.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Transcript export stats\n2: \n3: Generated: 2026-09-18T22:44:10Z\n4: \n5: Source directory: `hades-v2-memories/hades-v2/conversations/`\n6: \n7: This file is counts only. No message bodies, titles, paths, or display names.\n8: \n9: ## Sessions\n10: \n11: - Rows parsed: 1492\n12: - Bad JSONL lines: 0\n13: - Distinct session ids: 1492\n14: - Titles present (count only): 583\n15: - Started: 2026-06-29T16:04:09Z → 2026-08-11T03:20:53Z\n16: - Ended: 2026-06-29T16:09:36Z → 2026-08-04T02:54:35Z\n17: - Session `message_count` min / median / max / sum: 1 / 33 / 2560 / 90793\n18: - Session `tool_call_count` min / median / max / sum: 0 / 18 / 702 / 46783\n19: \n20: ### source\n21: \n22: | value | count |\n23: |---|---|\n24: | `subagent` | 701 |\n25: | `cron` | 512 |\n\n(Showing lines 1-25 of 127. Use offset=26 to continue.)\n</content>", "metadata": {"preview": "# Transcript export stats\n\nGenerated: 2026-09-18T22:44:10Z\n\nSource directory: `hades-v2-memories/hades-v2/conversations/`\n\nThis file is counts only. No message bodies, titles, paths, or display names.\n\n## Sessions\n\n- Rows parsed: 1492\n- Bad JSONL lines: 0\n- Distinct session ids: 1492\n- Titles present (count only): 583\n- Started: 2026-06-29T16:04:09Z → 2026-08-11T03:20:53Z\n- Ended: 2026-06-29T16:09:36Z → 2026-